Lab 19: Writing and Using Execution Environments
Objectives

By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

    Understand the concept and benefits of Ansible execution environments
    Create a custom execution environment definition using ansible-builder
    Build and test custom execution environments with a publicly accessible base image
    Configure and run Ansible playbooks using custom execution environments
    Troubleshoot common issues with execution environments
    Implement best practices for execution environment development, versioning, and validation

Prerequisites

Before starting this lab, you should have:

    Basic understanding of Ansible playbooks and modules
    Familiarity with Docker/Podman concepts and containerization
    Knowledge of Linux command line operations
    Understanding of YAML syntax
    Experience with package management (pip, yum/dnf)
    Basic knowledge of Python virtual environments

Required Software

This lab uses a CentOS/RHEL 8 or 9 based system with Docker installed. Install the required Python tooling with the exact commands below:

python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip
python3 -m pip install ansible-core ansible-builder ansible-runner

Verify the tools installed correctly:

ansible --version
ansible-builder --version
ansible-runner --version
docker --version

Important note on base images: This lab uses quay.io/centos/centos:stream9 as the base image for all execution environments. This image is publicly available on Quay.io and does not require authentication, unlike registry.redhat.io images which require a valid Red Hat subscription login. If you have Red Hat credentials and have run docker login registry.redhat.io, you may substitute registry.redhat.io/ubi8/ubi:latest or registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi:latest anywhere you see the CentOS Stream image, but the lab as written will work fully without any registry authentication.
