Conclusion

In this lab you built a complete Ansible execution environment workflow from the ground up, starting with a definition file based on the publicly available quay.io/centos/centos:stream9 image and progressing through Python, system, and collection dependency management with ansible-builder. You verified the resulting image by running functional test playbooks both directly with Docker and through ansible-runner, then extended the workflow by building separate development and production variants to see how build steps affect image size and content. Finally, you created validation and performance testing scripts that exercise the execution environment and produce concrete, checkable output confirming the environment behaves correctly under load. With these skills, you are prepared to design, build, and maintain execution environments for real Ansible Automation Platform deployments, including tagging strategies, private registries, and environment-specific dependency sets for larger automation projects.
